## 🕵️‍♂️ *The Harbor of Shadows*

The harbor smelled like salt, diesel, and secrets that didn’t want to stay buried.

I was leaning against a rusted lamppost, watching the fog roll in like it had something to confess, when she found me. They always do—trouble has a way of tracking me down like an unpaid debt.

“Are you Mercer?” she asked.

“Depends who’s asking,” I said, flicking ash into the wind. “And whether I owe you money.”

She didn’t smile. That was my first clue this wasn’t going to be a pleasant evening.

“Lila Vance,” she said. “My brother’s missing. Last seen here.”

I looked past her, out at the black water licking the docks. The Harbor of Shadows, they called it. Not on any official map, but everyone who mattered knew the name. Things disappeared here—cargo, people, sometimes whole truths.

“People don’t just vanish,” I said. “They leave, or they’re taken. Which is it?”

“That’s what I’m paying you to find out.”

---

Her brother, Daniel Vance. Dockworker. Clean record. No debts, no enemies—on paper, he was the kind of man who lived quiet and died quieter.

But paper lies.

I started with the night shift foreman, a man named Griggs who looked like he’d been carved out of old rope and bad decisions.

“Kid asked too many questions,” Griggs muttered. “Started poking around shipments that weren’t his business.”

“What kind of shipments?”

Griggs glanced at the water. “The kind that don’t exist.”

---

By midnight, I had a name: Calder. Ran the docks like a king with no crown and no conscience. If something illegal came through the harbor, Calder took his cut.

I found him in a warehouse that smelled like brine and fear.

“You’re digging where you shouldn’t,” Calder said, not bothering to look up from his glass.

“Occupational hazard,” I replied. “Guy goes missing, I get curious.”

Calder chuckled. “Curiosity’s a quick way to end up at the bottom of the harbor.”

“Yeah,” I said. “But it’s also how you find what people are trying to hide.”

---

The truth came cheap, in the end. It always does.

Daniel Vance hadn’t disappeared.

He’d discovered a smuggling ring—illegal goods moving through the harbor under fake manifests. He threatened to go to the police.

Calder had him silenced.

Simple. Clean. Final.

---

I found Lila where I’d left her, staring out at the water like it might give something back if she looked long enough.

“Well?” she asked.

I lit a cigarette, buying time I didn’t deserve.

“Your brother skipped town,” I said. “Found something he didn’t like and ran.”

She studied me. Really studied me. Like she was trying to see past the words.

“And you believe that?” she asked softly.

“No,” I said. “But it’s the story that keeps you safe.”

---

She didn’t thank me.

Didn’t argue, either.

Just nodded once, like she understood more than she let on, and walked away into the fog.

---

I went back to the harbor, to the place where the water runs deep and quiet.

Dropped the envelope Calder gave me into my coat pocket. Heavy. Enough to buy a little peace, if not a clean conscience.

The harbor didn’t care. It never does.

It just kept swallowing shadows.

And me?

I kept feeding it.
